config.prompt:
Set this work up so it can be worked at all, then report honestly on four conditions.

Task:
{{inputs.task}}

Working directory: {{inputs.cwd}}
The command that will decide done-ness: {{inputs.verify_command}}

Establish, and verify each by doing it rather than by reading about it:
  1. can_start — the environment runs: dependencies installed, the project builds.
  2. can_test — ONE test (or check) actually runs and passes right now. Not "there is a test suite" — you ran something and saw it pass.
  3. can_see_progress — {{inputs.verify_command}} runs and produces a readable pass/fail signal.
  4. can_pick_next — the task is broken into items, each with an acceptance criterion that could be checked by someone who did not write it.

Read enough of the code first that the breakdown reflects what is there rather than what would be convenient. An item naming a function that does not exist costs more than no plan.

Report a condition as false if it is false. The next step is a gate that blocks the work when any of them is, and it exists because starting to code in an environment that cannot run its own tests is the most expensive way to discover that.

Return JSON: {"can_start": boolean, "can_test": boolean, "can_see_progress": boolean, "can_pick_next": boolean, "blocked_by": string, "breakdown": [{"item": string, "acceptance": string}]}

Establish the failing check BEFORE anything is edited.

Task:
{{inputs.task}}
Bug fix: {{inputs.bug_flavored}}
Working directory: {{inputs.cwd}}

If this is a bug fix, NOTHING may be edited until a failing test, a failing command, or a documented reproduction exists. The only escape is that the failure depends on production data or infrastructure that cannot be simulated locally — effort alone does not qualify, and "I could not reproduce it but I can see the bug" is the shape this step exists to stop. The reproduction artifact IS the validation artifact: it is what proves the fix later.

If this is not a bug fix, write down the acceptance check that currently fails because the feature is absent, and set fail_reproduced to whether you saw it fail.

Return JSON: {"fail_reproduced": boolean, "repro_command": string, "observed": string, "infeasible_reason": string}

Implement exactly this one item, then stop.

Item: {{item.item}}
Acceptance: {{item.acceptance}}

The whole task, for context:
{{inputs.task}}

The reproduction this fix has to satisfy:
{{nodes.repro.output.repro_command}}
{{nodes.repro.output.observed}}

Match the surrounding code's idiom, naming and comment density — a file that reads as though a different person wrote one function is harder to maintain than a slightly worse but consistent one.

Expected values need an independent source of truth: a test that asserts what the code happens to do proves only that the code does what it does. Replace a wrong test rather than layering a second one beside it.

Do not touch {{inputs.verify_command}} or {{inputs.guard_command}}, and do not delete or skip a test to make either pass. A gate runs both commands after this and an independent judge checks for exactly those moves.

Run the test suite in {{inputs.cwd}} and report what happened, verbatim.

Run: {{inputs.guard_command}}
If that is empty, run: {{inputs.verify_command}}

Quote the failures exactly. A paraphrased failure cannot be triaged, and a summarized one hides which assertion broke.

Do not fix anything here. Do not re-run until it passes. This step exists to produce evidence.

Return JSON: {"passed": boolean, "failures": [string], "command_run": string}
